{
  "dom_id": "step-q12341",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the point of intersection of the lines $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$",
  "worked_solution": "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=-3x+4$ and $y=3x-2.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-3x+4 \\\\ y=3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=-3x+4$ into the second equation $y=3x-2$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4 & =3x-2 \\\\ -6x & =-6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(1)-2 \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=1.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are  $(1,1).$",
